    "content": "{\n  \"files\": [\n    {\n      \"content\": \"#include <stdio.h>\\n#include <stdlib.h>\\n#include <string.h>\\n#include <stdbool.h>\\n\\ntypedef struct {\\n    float x;\\n    float y;\\n} Point;\\n\\ntypedef struct {\\n    char label;\\n    int length;\\n    Point * points;\\n} Group;\\n\\nstatic char *input_data = NULL;\\nstatic size_t input_pos = 0;\\nstatic size_t input_len = 0;\\n\\nvoid on_error() {\\n    printf(\\\"Invalid input file.\\\\n\\\");\\n    exit(1);\\n}\\n\\nstatic void read_all_input() {\\n    if (input_data) return;\\n    long sz = -1;\\n    if (!fseek(stdin, 0, SEEK_END)) {\\n        sz = ftell(stdin);\\n        rewind(stdin);\\n    }\\n    if (sz > 0) {\\n        input_data = (char*)malloc(sz ? sz : 1);\\n        size_t r = fread(input_data, 1, sz, stdin);\\n        if (r != (size_t)sz && !feof(stdin)) on_error();\\n        input_len = r;\\n    } else {\\n        size_t cap = 1<<20;\\n        input_data = (char*)malloc(cap);\\n        int c; size_t n=0;\\n        while ((c=getchar())!=EOF) {\\n            if(n+1>=cap){cap*=2; input_data=(char*)realloc(input_data,cap);}\\n            input_data[n++]=(char)c;\\n        }\\n        input_len=n;\\n    }\\n    input_pos=0;\\n}\\n\\nstatic void skip_ws() {\\n    while (input_pos < input_len) {\\n        char c = input_data[input_pos];\\n        if(c==' '||c=='\\\\n'||c=='\\\\t'||c=='\\\\r') input_pos++;\\n        else break;\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nstatic int parse_int() {\\n    skip_ws();\\n    int sign=1, val=0; bool seen=false;\\n    if(input_pos<input_len && (input_data[input_pos]=='-'||input_data[input_pos]=='+')){if(input_data[input_pos]=='-')sign=-1; input_pos++;}\\n    while(input_pos<input_len && input_data[input_pos]>='0'&&input_data[input_pos]<='9'){\\n        val=val*10+(input_data[input_pos]-'0'); input_pos++; seen=true;\\n    }\\n    if(!seen) on_error();\\n    return sign*val;\\n}\\n\\nstatic float parse_float() {\\n    skip_ws();\\n    int sign=1; double val=0.0; bool seen=false;\\n    if(input_pos<input_len && (input_data[input_pos]=='-'||input_data[input_pos]=='+')){if(input_data[input_pos]=='-')sign=-1; input_pos++;}\\n    while(input_pos<input_len && input_data[input_pos]>='0'&&input_data[input_pos]<='9'){\\n        val=val*10+(input_data[input_pos]-'0'); input_pos++; seen=true;\\n    }\\n    if(input_pos<input_len && input_data[input_pos]=='.'){\\n        input_pos++;\\n        double frac=1.0;\\n        while(input_pos<input_len && input_data[input_pos]>='0'&&input_data[input_pos]<='9'){\\n            frac/=10; val += (input_data[input_pos]-'0')*frac; input_pos++; seen=true;\\n        }\\n    }\\n    if(!seen) on_error();\\n    return sign*(float)val;\\n}\\n\\nint parse_number_of_groups() {\\n    read_all_input();\\n    skip_ws();\\n    const char *needle = \\\"n_groups=\\\";\\n    size_t nl = strlen(needle);\\n    if (input_pos + nl > input_len || strncmp(input_data+input_pos, needle, nl)!=0) on_error();\\n    input_pos += nl;\\n    return parse_int();\\n}\\n\\nPoint parse_point() {\\n    read_all_input();\\n    skip_ws();\\n    if(input_pos>=input_len || input_data[input_pos]!='(') on_error();\\n    input_pos++;\\n    float x = parse_float();\\n    skip_ws();\\n    if(input_pos>=input_len || input_data[input_pos]!=',') on_error();\\n    input_pos++;\\n    float y = parse_float();\\n    skip_ws();\\n    if(input_pos>=input_len || input_data[input_pos]!=')') on_error();\\n    input_pos++;\\n    Point p; p.x=x; p.y=y; return p;\\n}\\n\\nGroup parse_next_group() {\\n    read_all_input();\\n    char label;\\n    skip_ws();\\n    const char *lbl = \\\"label=\\\";\\n    size_t ll = strlen(lbl);\\n    if (input_pos + ll > input_len || strncmp(input_data+input_pos, lbl, ll)!=0) on_error();\\n    input_pos += ll;\\n    skip_ws();\\n    if(input_pos>=input_len) on_error();\\n    label = input_data[input_pos++];\\n\\n    skip_ws();\\n    const char *len_str = \\\"length=\\\";\\n    size_t lls = strlen(len_str);\\n    if (input_pos + lls > input_len || strncmp(input_data+input_pos, len_str, lls)!=0) on_error();\\n    input_pos += lls;\\n    int length = parse_int();\\n\\n    Group g; g.label=label; g.length=length;\\n    g.points = (Point*)malloc(sizeof(Point)*length);\\n    for(int i=0;i<length;i++) g.points[i]=parse_point();\\n    return g;\\n}\\n\\nint parse_k() {\\n    read_all_input();\\n    skip_ws();\\n    const char *needle = \\\"k=\\\";\\n    size_t nl = strlen(needle);\\n    if (input_pos + nl > input_len || strncmp(input_data+input_pos, needle, nl)!=0) on_error();\\n    input_pos += nl;\\n    return parse_int();\\n}\\n\\nfloat euclidean_distance_no_sqrt (Point a, Point b) {\\n    float dx = b.x - a.x;\\n    float dy = b.y - a.y;\\n    return dx*dx + dy*dy;\\n}\\n\\nint compare_for_sort(const void *a, const void *b) {\\n  return *(char*)a - *(char*)b;\\n}\\n\",\n      \"path\": \"src/knn.h\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"content\": \"#include \\\"knn.h\\\"\\n#include <omp.h>\\n#include <float.h>\\n\\nchar knn(int n_groups, Group * groups, int k, Point to_evaluate) {\\n    long total = 0;\\n    for (int i=0;i<n_groups;i++) total += groups[i].length;\\n\\n    char *flat_labels = (char*)malloc(total ? total : 1);\\n    Point *flat_points = (Point*)malloc(sizeof(Point)*(total?total:1));\\n    long pos=0;\\n    for(int gi=0;gi<n_groups;gi++){\\n        Group g = groups[gi];\\n        for(int pi=0;pi<g.length;pi++){\\n            flat_labels[pos]=g.label;\\n            flat_points[pos]=g.points[pi];\\n            pos++;\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    int nthreads = omp_get_max_threads();\\n    float **ldists = (float**)malloc(sizeof(float*)*nthreads);\\n    char **llabels = (char**)malloc(sizeof(char*)*nthreads);\\n    long **lidx = (long**)malloc(sizeof(long*)*nthreads);\\n    for(int t=0;t<nthreads;t++){\\n        ldists[t]=(float*)malloc(sizeof(float)*k);\\n        llabels[t]=(char*)malloc(sizeof(char)*k);\\n        lidx[t]=(long*)malloc(sizeof(long)*k);\\n        for(int j=0;j<k;j++){ldists[t][j]=FLT_MAX; llabels[t][j]=-1; lidx[t][j]=-1;}\\n    }\\n\\n    #pragma omp parallel\\n    {\\n        int tid = omp_get_thread_num();\\n        float *ld = ldists[tid];\\n        char *ll = llabels[tid];\\n        long *li = lidx[tid];\\n\\n        #pragma omp for schedule(static)\\n        for(long idx=0; idx<total; idx++){\\n            float d = euclidean_distance_no_sqrt(to_evaluate, flat_points[idx]);\\n            int x;\\n            for(x=0;x<k;x++){\\n                if(d < ld[x] || ld[x]==FLT_MAX){\\n                    for(int y=k-1;y>x;y--){\\n                        ld[y]=ld[y-1];\\n                        ll[y]=ll[y-1];\\n                        li[y]=li[y-1];\\n                    }\\n                    ld[x]=d;\\n                    ll[x]=flat_labels[idx];\\n                    li[x]=idx;\\n                    break;\\n                }\\n            }\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    float *gdist = (float*)malloc(sizeof(float)*k);\\n    char *glabel = (char*)malloc(sizeof(char)*k);\\n    for(int i=0;i<k;i++){ gdist[i]=FLT_MAX; glabel[i]=-1; }\\n    int *ptr = (int*)calloc(nthreads, sizeof(int));\\n    int filled=0;\\n    while(filled < k){\\n        int best_t=-1;\\n        float best_d=FLT_MAX;\\n        long best_idx=-1;\\n        for(int t=0;t<nthreads;t++){\\n            if(ptr[t] < k && ldists[t][ptr[t]] != FLT_MAX){\\n                float d = ldists[t][ptr[t]];\\n                long idx = lidx[t][ptr[t]];\\n                if(d < best_d || (d==best_d && idx < best_idx)){\\n                    best_d=d; best_idx=idx; best_t=t;\\n                }\\n            }\\n        }\\n        if(best_t==-1) break;\\n        gdist[filled]=best_d;\\n        glabel[filled]=llabels[best_t][ptr[best_t]];\\n        ptr[best_t]++;\\n        filled++;\\n    }\\n\\n    char *labels = (char*)malloc(sizeof(char)*k);\\n    for(int i=0;i<k;i++) labels[i] = glabel[i];\\n\\n    qsort(labels, k, sizeof(char), compare_for_sort);\\n\\n    char most_frequent = labels[0];\\n    int most_frequent_count = 1;\\n    int current_frequency = 1;\\n\\n    for (int i = 1; i < k; i++) {\\n        if (labels[i] != labels[i - 1]) {\\n            if (current_frequency > most_frequent_count) {\\n                most_frequent = labels[i - 1];\\n                most_frequent_count = current_frequency;\\n            }\\n            current_frequency = 1;\\n        } else {\\n            current_frequency++;\\n        }\\n\\n        if (i == k - 1 && current_frequency > most_frequent_count) {\\n            most_frequent = labels[i - 1];\\n            most_frequent_count = current_frequency;\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    free(flat_labels); free(flat_points);\\n    for(int t=0;t<nthreads;t++){free(ldists[t]); free(llabels[t]); free(lidx[t]);}\\n    free(ldists); free(llabels); free(lidx);\\n    free(gdist); free(glabel); free(ptr); free(labels);\\n\\n    return most_frequent;\\n}\\n\\nint main() {\\n    int n_groups = parse_number_of_groups();\\n    \\n    Group * groups = (Group *) malloc(sizeof(Group) * n_groups);\\n\\n    for (int i = 0; i < n_groups; i++) {\\n        groups[i] = parse_next_group();\\n    }\\n\\n    int k = parse_k();\\n\\n    Point to_evaluate = parse_point();\\n\\n    printf(\\\"%c\\\", knn(n_groups, groups, k, to_evaluate));\\n}\\n\",\n      \"path\": \"src/knn.c\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hypothesis\": \"Parallelize KNN top-k selection across points using OpenMP static work sharing, each thread maintaining a private sorted list; merge deterministically by (distance, original index) to preserve exact sequential tie-breaking. Replace scanf-based parsing with a custom buffered parser to reduce I/O overhead.\",\n  \"notes\": \"The parallel algorithm relies on the equivalence between the sequential insertion order and lexicographic ordering of (squared distance, point index). Each thread processes a contiguous chunk under static scheduling; per-thread lists are merged by comparing both fields. The fast parser reads all input into memory once and manually extracts numbers, eliminating scanf overhead.\",\n  \"sources_kept\": [],\n  \"transformations\": [\n    \"Replaced scanf-based parsing with a custom buffered manual parser in knn.h\",\n    \"Flattened groups/points into contiguous arrays for cache-friendly access\",\n    \"Added OpenMP parallel-for over all points with static scheduling and per-thread private top-k lists\",\n    \"Implemented deterministic merge of per-thread lists using (distance, original index) ordering to preserve exact tie-breaking semantics\"\n  ]\n}",
    "thinking": "We need to propose one bounded HPC source-code experiment. We are role C: OpenMP work sharing, reductions, scheduling and thread granularity. The current sources are knn.c and knn.h. We can modify only these files under src/. Need to produce complete contents of changed files.\n\nGoal: improve performance (speedup) while preserving exact output bytes against official reference. Baseline mean 2.085s; best observed in interactions ~0.0286s with score 0.9865, from some combination of sources (6047c2dead114efa etc). We don't have those source contents directly but we can infer likely optimizations: custom fast parser, parallelization.\n\nWe need to propose one candidate relevant to role C: OpenMP work sharing, reductions, scheduling and thread granularity. So focus on parallelizing the KNN computation using OpenMP with appropriate scheduling and reduction/private lists.\n\nCurrent code is sequential. We can implement a parallel version that preserves exact semantics (ties broken by original order? Actually output only label of most frequent among k nearest; ties in distance are handled by insertion order: if d < distances[x] or distances[x]==-1, it inserts at first position where condition true, shifting later elements. This means for equal distances, earlier points (in iteration order) get inserted before later ones? Let's analyze.\n\nSequential algorithm maintains sorted list of k smallest distances with labels. For each point in input order (groups sequential, within group points sequential), compute d. Then find first x such that d < distances[x] or distances[x]==-1; if found, shift elements from x to k-2 right and insert at x. This means for equal distance d, a new point with same d will be inserted before existing entries with distance == d? Because condition is d < distances[x], not <=. So if distances[x] == d, it does NOT satisfy d < distances[x]; so it goes to later position where either d < next or -1. Thus for equal distances, earlier points are kept ahead of later ones (stable in terms of insertion order). This affects which k points are selected when there are ties at the boundary; but output only depends on labels frequencies among selected k. However exact byte comparison requires same label selection as reference sequential implementation.\n\nIf we parallelize by splitting points across threads, each thread maintains its own top-k list for its subset. Then merging per-thread lists must reproduce exactly what sequential would produce if all points processed in original order. This is tricky because ties and insertion semantics depend on global order. But maybe the official tests don't have boundary ties? Or we can design merge that preserves exact behavior.\n\nAlternative: Use OpenMP to parallelize distance computation but keep top-k selection serial? That wouldn't help much because top-k is O(N*k) with k small? Actually N could be large, k maybe moderate. The sequential time 2s suggests heavy input parsing and/or many points. Best observed ~0.0286s indicates huge speedup from custom parser + parallelization.\n\nWe need to propose one candidate. We can use OpenMP work sharing on the point loop with per-thread private top-k lists, then merge deterministically preserving original order semantics. But merging is complex. Another approach: Use a reduction-like strategy where each thread computes partial distances and we combine using a global priority queue? Or use parallel sorting of all points by distance? That would be O(N log N) but maybe faster with OpenMP.\n\nGiven role C specifically about work sharing, reductions, scheduling, thread granularity, likely expected to add #pragma omp parallel for on the point loop with private arrays and then merge. But we need correctness.\n\nLet's inspect current code: knn.c includes knn.h which has parsing functions using scanf. That is slow. We can replace parser with custom fast integer/float parser reading whole stdin buffer. Also parallelize top-k selection.\n\nWe have seen previous own role experiments: 571a2dc509d041d3 achieved 0.5527s, aee01de8e6e34175 0.6999s, 63943000884b4332 1.175s. The best overall interactions include source IDs like 6047c2dead114efa etc with times ~0.0286s and score 0.9865. Those likely combined custom parser + OpenMP.\n\nWe need to propose one candidate. We can implement a robust parallel version:\n\n- Use fast buffered input parsing (fread whole file into memory, parse manually).\n- Store all points in contiguous arrays: labels array of char per point? Actually each group has label and length; we can flatten.\n- Parallelize distance computation and top-k selection using OpenMP with static scheduling over flattened point index. Each thread maintains its own local top-k arrays (distances and labels) initialized to -1. After parallel loop, merge the per-thread lists into a global list preserving exact sequential semantics.\n\nBut merging exactly is hard. However we can instead use an alternative approach: compute all distances in parallel into an array of structs {distance, label, original_index}, then sort this array by distance (and maybe original index for ties) using OpenMP parallel sort? But sorting N elements with qsort might be O(N log N), but if k is small relative to N, we only need top-k. We could use a parallel selection algorithm or each thread finds local top-k and merge.\n\nGiven the sequential insertion semantics: For equal distances, earlier points are preferred (since condition d < distances[x] not <=). So when merging per-thread lists, we must consider original order for ties. If we assign contiguous chunks to threads in static scheduling with increasing index, then within each thread's list, entries are sorted by distance and for equal distance, lower original index comes first? Actually insertion algorithm maintains that if two points have same distance d, the one processed earlier (lower global index) will be at a smaller position because when later point arrives, it won't replace an existing entry with same d; it goes after. So within each thread's local list, for equal distances, entries are in increasing original order. When merging multiple threads' lists, we need to merge by distance and then by original index (since global order is contiguous chunks: all points from thread 0 have lower indices than thread 1 etc). So if two candidates have same distance, the one with smaller original index should come first. This can be achieved by comparing (distance, original_index) lexicographically.\n\nBut we also need to handle -1 sentinel for empty slots. We'll initialize local lists with distances = INFINITY? Actually sequential uses -1 as \"empty\" and condition d < distances[x] || distances[x]==-1; since distances are non-negative squared Euclidean (>=0), -1 is less than any real distance, so it acts like infinity but insertion logic: if distances[x]==-1, insert there. So we can use FLT_MAX or INFINITY as sentinel and condition d < distances[x] || distances[x]==INFINITY? But careful: sequential uses -1; for merging we need to treat empty slots as larger than any real distance. We'll set local distances to INFINITY (or a large float) and labels to something.\n\nSimpler: Each thread maintains its own top-k list using same insertion algorithm but with sentinel = FLT_MAX? Actually sequential uses -1; if we use FLT_MAX, condition d < distances[x] || distances[x]==FLT_MAX works. But when merging, empty slots should be considered as \"infinite\" so they go last.\n\nWe can implement per-thread local arrays of size k (distances and labels). After parallel loop, each thread has a sorted list of up to k entries with increasing distance; for equal distances, lower original index first because insertion stable. Then we merge these lists into global top-k by repeatedly picking the smallest (distance, original_index) among all threads' current heads. But we need original_index stored per entry.\n\nWe can store in local arrays also an int idx array to keep original point index. Then after parallel loop, each thread's list is sorted by distance then idx. We merge using a priority queue or simple linear scan over t lists (t <= 4 threads). Since k may be small? Unknown but likely moderate. We'll do O(t*k) merging.\n\nBut we need to ensure that the global top-k exactly matches sequential selection. Let's prove: Sequential processes points in order 0..N-1, maintaining sorted list by distance with stable insertion (earlier index first for equal distances). This is equivalent to selecting k smallest elements according to lexicographic ordering (distance, original_index) because if two points have same distance, the one with smaller index appears earlier and thus will be kept over later ones when capacity full. Actually consider sequential: It keeps at most k entries; when a new point arrives, it inserts only if its distance is less than some existing entry's distance OR there is an empty slot. If equal to an existing distance, it does not replace that entry (since d < distances[x] false), so it goes after all entries with same or smaller distance? Let's simulate: Suppose current list has [d=1 idx0], new point d=1 idx5. It checks x=0: 1<1 false; next x maybe empty -> insert at end. So list becomes [idx0, idx5]. If later another d=1 idx2 arrives, it will check x=0 false, x=1? distances[1]=1, 1<1 false, then empty -> insert after both. So order among equal distance is insertion order (increasing index). Thus final top-k consists of k smallest by (distance, original_index) lexicographically. Because if there are more than k points with same minimal distance, the first k in index order are kept. This matches sorting all points by (d, idx) and taking first k.\n\nTherefore we can compute distances for all points in parallel, store them in arrays dist[i], label[i] (or group label), then find top-k using a parallel selection or sort. But sorting N elements might be expensive but maybe still faster than sequential O(N*k)? If N is large and k small, sorting O(N log N) could be slower. Better to use per-thread local top-k with merge as described.\n\nWe'll implement:\n\n- Parse input fast.\n- Flatten points: create arrays `float *all_dists`? Actually we can compute distances on the fly in parallel loop; but need original index for tie-breaking and label. We'll store labels array of char length N, and maybe coordinates? But we can just iterate over groups/points directly with OpenMP.\n\nSimpler: In knn function, instead of nested loops, create a flattened list of points with their group label. Then parallelize over that flat index.\n\nWe need to modify knn.c only; knn.h contains parsing functions and euclidean_distance_no_sqrt. We can keep those but maybe replace parser in knn.h? The instructions say edit source files under src/, we can change both .c and .h. But careful: other experiments may rely on header structure? We are allowed to modify.\n\nWe'll propose a candidate that:\n\n1. Replaces scanf-based parsing with custom fast parser using fread.\n2. Parallelizes the top-k selection using OpenMP static scheduling over flattened points, each thread maintains private local arrays of size k (distances, labels, indices). After loop, merge deterministically by (distance, index) to produce global top-k.\n\nWe need to ensure correctness for all inputs including ties and varying k, n_groups. Also handle cases where total points < k? Sequential would still work: distances array initialized -1; if fewer than k points, some slots remain -1; labels -1; after qsort of labels (char) with -1 values, most_frequent might be -1? But input likely has enough points.\n\nLet's design code carefully.\n\nWe'll modify knn.h to include fast parser functions and maybe keep euclidean_distance_no_sqrt. We can add a function `read_all_input` that parses into n_groups, groups etc but we need to preserve interface for main in knn.c? Actually main calls parse_number_of_groups(), parse_next_group(), parse_k(), parse_point(). We could replace those functions with fast versions using global buffer.\n\nBetter: rewrite entire parsing in knn.h as static inline or just implement a custom `fast_read` that reads all input into memory and parses. But we need to keep the same function signatures because main uses them. We can change implementations of parse_number_of_groups, parse_next_group, parse_k, parse_point to use a global buffer pointer.\n\nLet's design:\n\nIn knn.h:\n- Add `#include <string.h>` maybe.\n- Define static char *input_buf; size_t input_len; size_t pos;\n- Function `init_input()` that reads all stdin into malloc'd buffer using fread.\n- Then parse functions use sscanf? No, we want fast manual parsing. We can implement simple skip whitespace and parse int/float.\n\nBut floats in input format: \"(%f,%f)\" with possible spaces. We need to handle exactly as scanf does (skips leading whitespace). The official reference uses scanf with formats like \" n_groups=%d \", which skips whitespace before literal? Actually `scanf(\" n_groups=%d \")` will skip any whitespace, then match literal \"n_groups=\", then read int, then skip trailing whitespace. We can replicate by scanning for '=' etc.\n\nWe'll implement a simple parser that reads characters and extracts numbers.\n\nGiven time constraints, maybe we don't need to replace parser; the main speedup might come from parallelization alone? But baseline 2s likely dominated by scanf overhead. Best observed ~0.0286 suggests custom parser is crucial. So include fast parser.\n\nLet's write code:\n\nIn knn.h:\n```\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\ntypedef struct { float x; float y; } Point;\ntypedef struct { char label; int length; Point * points; } Group;\n\nstatic char *input_data = NULL;\nstatic size_t input_pos = 0;\nstatic size_t input_len = 0;\n\nvoid on_error() { printf(\"Invalid input file.\\n\"); exit(1); }\n\nstatic void read_all_input() {\n    if (input_data) return;\n    // determine size\n    fseek(stdin, 0, SEEK_END);\n    long sz = ftell(stdin);\n    rewind(stdin);\n    if (sz < 0) { // fallback to reading chunks\n        input_len = 1<<20;\n        input_data = malloc(input_len);\n        size_t total=0; int c;\n        while ((c=getchar())!=EOF) {\n            if(total+1>=input_len){input_len*=2; input_data=realloc(input_data,input_len);}\n            input_data[total++]=(char)c;\n        }\n        input_len=total;\n    } else {\n        input_data = malloc(sz ? sz : 1);\n        size_t r = fread(input_data,1,sz,stdin);\n        if (r != (size_t)sz && !feof(stdin)) on_error();\n        input_len = r;\n    }\n    input_pos=0;\n}\n\nstatic void skip_ws() {\n    while (input_pos < input_len && (input_data[input_pos]==' '||input_data[input_pos]=='\\n'||input_data[input_pos]=='\\t'||input_data[input_pos]=='\\r')) input_pos++;\n}\n```\n\nBut fseek on stdin may not work if pipe? Usually works. We'll use getchar fallback.\n\nThen parse functions:\n\n```\nint parse_number_of_groups() {\n    read_all_input();\n    skip_ws();\n    // expect \"n_groups=\"\n    const char *needle = \"n_groups=\";\n    size_t nl = strlen(needle);\n    if (input_pos + nl > input_len || strncmp(input_data+input_pos, needle, nl)!=0) on_error();\n    input_pos += nl;\n    int n=0; skip_ws(); while(input_pos<input_len && input_data[input_pos]>='0'&&input_data[input_pos]<='9'){n=n*10+(input_data[input_pos]-'0'); input_pos++;}\n    return n;\n}\n```\n\nBut there may be spaces around '='? The format \" n_groups=%d \" means after skipping whitespace, literal \"n_groups=\" then optional whitespace before number. We'll handle.\n\nSimilarly parse_point: skip_ws, expect '(', read float x, skip until ',', read float y, expect ')'. Need to parse floats with possible negative and decimal. Implement a helper `parse_float`.\n\nWe can implement:\n\n```\nstatic float parse_float() {\n    skip_ws();\n    int sign=1;\n    if(input_pos<input_len && (input_data[input_pos]=='-'||input_data[input_pos]=='+')){if(input_data[input_pos]=='-')sign=-1; input_pos++;}\n    double val=0.0; bool seen=false;\n    while(input_pos<input_len && input_data[input_pos]>='0'&&input_data[input_pos]<='9'){val=val*10+(input_data[input_pos]-'0'); input_pos++; seen=true;}\n    if(input_pos<input_len && input_data[input_pos]=='.'){\n        input_pos++;\n        double frac=1.0;\n        while(input_pos<input_len && input_data[input_pos]>='0'&&input_data[input_pos]<='9'){frac/=10; val += (input_data[input_pos]-'0')*frac; input_pos++; seen=true;}\n    }\n    if(!seen) on_error();\n    return sign*(float)val;\n}\n```\n\nNeed to include <stdbool.h> or use int.\n\nThen parse_point:\n```\nPoint parse_point() {\n    read_all_input(); // already called\n    skip_ws();\n    if(input_pos>=input_len || input_data[input_pos]!='(') on_error();\n    input_pos++;\n    float x = parse_float();\n    skip_ws();\n    if(input_pos>=input_len || input_data[input_pos]!=',') on_error();\n    input_pos++;\n    float y = parse_float();\n    skip_ws();\n    if(input_pos>=input_len || input_data[input_pos]!=')') on_error();\n    input_pos++;\n    Point p; p.x=x; p.y=y; return p;\n}\n```\n\nparse_next_group:\n```\nGroup parse_next_group() {\n    read_all_input();\n    char label;\n    skip_ws();\n    // expect \"label=\"\n    if(input_pos+6>input_len || strncmp(input_data+input_pos,\"label=\",6)!=0) on_error();\n    input_pos+=6;\n    skip_ws();\n    if(input_pos>=input_len) on_error();\n    label = input_data[input_pos++];\n    // then length=\n    skip_ws();\n    const char *len_str=\"length=\";\n    size_t ll=strlen(len_str);\n    if(input_pos+ll>input_len || strncmp(input_data+input_pos,len_str,ll)!=0) on_error();\n    input_pos+=ll;\n    int length=0; skip_ws(); while(input_pos<input_len && input_data[input_pos]>='0'&&input_data[input_pos]<='9'){length=length*10+(input_data[input_pos]-'0'); input_pos++;}\n    Group g; g.label=label; g.length=length;\n    g.points = (Point*)malloc(sizeof(Point)*length);\n    for(int i=0;i<length;i++) g.points[i]=parse_point();\n    return g;\n}\n```\n\nparse_k similar.\n\nBut note: parse_number_of_groups is called first, then loop calls parse_next_group. Each call to read_all_input will be no-op after first. Good.\n\nNow knn.c parallelization:\n\nWe'll rewrite knn function to use OpenMP. We need to include <omp.h> and maybe <float.h>.\n\nPseudo:\n\n```\nchar knn(int n_groups, Group * groups, int k, Point to_evaluate) {\n    // Count total points\n    long total = 0;\n    for (int i=0;i<n_groups;i++) total += groups[i].length;\n\n    // Allocate arrays for distances and labels? We'll compute on fly.\n    // Use OpenMP parallel for over flattened index.\n    int nthreads = omp_get_max_threads();\n    float *local_dists[nthreads];\n    char *local_labels[nthreads];\n    long *local_idx[nthreads]; // original point index\n    // allocate each local array of size k, initialize to INFINITY and -1 label? We'll use FLT_MAX.\n    for (int t=0;t<nthreads;t++) {\n        local_dists[t] = malloc(sizeof(float)*k);\n        local_labels[t] = malloc(sizeof(char)*k);\n        local_idx[t] = malloc(sizeof(long)*k);\n        for(int j=0;j<k;j++){local_dists[t][j]=FLT_MAX; local_labels[t][j]=-1; local_idx[t][j]=-1;}\n    }\n\n    #pragma omp parallel\n    {\n        int tid = omp_get_thread_num();\n        float *ld = local_dists[tid];\n        char *ll = local_labels[tid];\n        long *li = local_idx[tid];\n\n        #pragma omp for schedule(static) nowait? Actually we need all threads finish before merge. Use default barrier.\n        for (long idx=0; idx<total; idx++) {\n            // find group and point\n            int gi, pi;\n            // We can precompute offsets: cumulative lengths array.\n            // But inside loop we'd have to search. Better flatten points into arrays first? Or use a mapping from flat index to group/point by scanning groups each time O(n_groups) per point -> bad.\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nBetter to create flattened arrays before parallel region:\n\n- `char *flat_labels = malloc(total)`; `Point *flat_points = malloc(sizeof(Point)*total);` Fill in sequential loop over groups.\n\nThen parallel for over flat index, compute distance and insert into local list using same insertion logic but with sentinel FLT_MAX. We need to store original idx (which is just the flat index) for tie-breaking during merge.\n\nInsertion function per thread:\n\n```\nfor (int x=0;x<k;x++) {\n    if (d < ld[x] || ld[x]==FLT_MAX) { // sentinel\n        for (int y=k-1;y>x;y--) { ld[y]=ld[y-1]; ll[y]=ll[y-1]; li[y]=li[y-1]; }\n        ld[x]=d; ll[x]=flat_labels[idx]; li[x]=idx;\n        break;\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThis matches sequential semantics within the thread's subset. Since each thread processes a contiguous chunk (static schedule), local list is sorted by distance then idx.\n\nAfter parallel loop, merge:\n\nWe have nthreads lists each of size k (some may be all FLT_MAX if fewer points). We need to select global top-k from these sorted lists according to lexicographic (distance, index). Since each list is sorted ascending by distance and for equal distance by index (because insertion stable), we can merge using a simple algorithm: maintain pointers p[t] starting at 0. For i in 0..k-1:\n- Find thread t with smallest (ld[p[t]], li[p[t]]) among those where ld[p[t]] != FLT_MAX.\n- If none, break (not enough points).\n- Assign global_dist[i]=..., global_label[i]=...\n- Increment p[t].\n\nBut we need to be careful: The local lists may have fewer than k real entries; sentinel FLT_MAX with idx -1. We'll treat those as \"infinite\" so they won't be selected until all real ones are exhausted.\n\nAfter merging, we have global arrays of size k (distances and labels). Then count frequencies exactly like sequential: qsort labels? Actually sequential sorts labels after top-k selection; but sorting char array with -1 sentinel might affect if fewer than k points. We'll replicate same logic: copy labels to a new array `char *final_labels = malloc(k)` from merged labels, then qsort and count.\n\nBut note: The merge must produce exactly the same set of labels as sequential would select. Since we argued that selecting top-k by (distance, idx) is equivalent, this should be correct.\n\nPotential issue: If there are ties at boundary where multiple points have same distance and some are excluded due to index order; our merge picks smallest indices first for equal distances because each local list has stable order and merging compares idx when distances equal. So global selection will pick the k smallest (distance, idx) pairs. That matches sequential.\n\nLet's test with example: Suppose two threads, thread0 points 0..4, thread1 points 5..9. If distance values tie across boundary, e.g., point3 d=2, point7 d=2, and k=1. Sequential would process point3 first (idx3) then point7; since equal, it keeps idx3 because insertion condition d < distances[0]? Initially empty -> insert idx3. Then point7: d==distances[0], so not less, goes to next slot but k=1 no slot, so discarded. So selected idx3. Our merge: thread0 local list has [idx3,d2]; thread1 local list has [idx7,d2]. Merge picks smallest (d,idx): both d equal, compare idx 3<7 -> pick idx3. Good.\n\nIf k=2 and there are three points with same distance indices 1,5,9; sequential keeps first two: 1 then 5 (since after inserting 1, point5 goes to second slot because not less than distances[0], but empty at x=1 so insert; point9 sees d==distances[0] and d==distances[1], no empty -> discard). Our merge picks idx1 then idx5. Good.\n\nSo algorithm correct.\n\nNow we need to implement flattening efficiently. We'll modify knn.c:\n\n```\n#include \"knn.h\"\n#include <omp.h>\n#include <float.h>\n\nchar knn(int n_groups, Group * groups, int k, Point to_evaluate) {\n    long total = 0;\n    for (int i=0;i<n_groups;i++) total += groups[i].length;\n\n    // Flatten\n    char *flat_labels = malloc(total);\n    Point *flat_points = malloc(sizeof(Point)*total);\n    long pos=0;\n    for (int gi=0;gi<n_groups;gi++) {\n        Group g = groups[gi];\n        for(int pi=0;pi<g.length;pi++){\n            flat_labels[pos]=g.label;\n            flat_points[pos]=g.points[pi];\n            pos++;\n        }\n    }\n\n    int nthreads = omp_get_max_threads();\n    // allocate per-thread arrays\n    float **ldists = malloc(sizeof(float*)*nthreads);\n    char **llabels = malloc(sizeof(char*)*nthreads);\n    long **lidx = malloc(sizeof(long*)*nthreads);\n    for(int t=0;t<nthreads;t++){\n        ldists[t]=malloc(sizeof(float)*k);\n        llabels[t]=malloc(sizeof(char)*k);\n        lidx[t]=malloc(sizeof(long)*k);\n        for(int j=0;j<k;j++){ldists[t][j]=FLT_MAX; llabels[t][j]=-1; lidx[t][j]=-1;}\n    }\n\n    #pragma omp parallel\n    {\n        int tid = omp_get_thread_num();\n        float *ld = ldists[tid];\n        char *ll = llabels[tid];\n        long *li = lidx[tid];\n\n        #pragma omp for schedule(static)\n        for(long idx=0; idx<total; idx++){\n            float d = euclidean_distance_no_sqrt(to_evaluate, flat_points[idx]);\n            // insert\n            int x;\n            for(x=0;x<k;x++){\n                if(d < ld[x] || ld[x]==FLT_MAX){\n                    for(int y=k-1;y>x;y--){\n                        ld[y]=ld[y-1];\n                        ll[y]=ll[y-1];\n                        li[y]=li[y-1];\n                    }\n                    ld[x]=d;\n                    ll[x]=flat_labels[idx];\n                    li[x]=idx;\n                    break;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Merge\n    float *gdist = malloc(sizeof(float)*k);\n    char *glabel = malloc(sizeof(char)*k);\n    long *gidx = malloc(sizeof(long)*k); // not needed after but for merge\n    int *ptr = calloc(nthreads, sizeof(int));\n    for(int i=0;i<k;i++){\n        int best_t=-1;\n        float best_d=FLT_MAX;\n        long best_idx=-1;\n        for(int t=0;t<nthreads;t++){\n            if(ptr[t] < k && ldists[t][ptr[t]] != FLT_MAX){\n                float d = ldists[t][ptr[t]];\n                long idx = lidx[t][ptr[t]];\n                if(d < best_d || (d==best_d && idx < best_idx)){\n                    best_d=d; best_idx=idx; best_t=t;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        if(best_t==-1) break; // not enough points\n        gdist[i]=best_d;\n        glabel[i]=llabels[best_t][ptr[best_t]];\n        ptr[best_t]++;\n    }\n\n    // Count frequencies exactly like original\n    char *labels = malloc(sizeof(char)*k);\n    for(int i=0;i<k;i++) labels[i] = (i < total ? glabel[i] : -1); // but if fewer than k, sequential would have -1 in remaining slots. Our merge loop breaks early leaving uninitialized; we should initialize all to -1.\n```\n\nActually original initializes distances and labels arrays of size k with -1 before processing points. If total < k, after processing all points some entries remain -1. Then qsort sorts them (char) including -1 values. The frequency counting then may pick most_frequent as -1 if no real label appears more than once? But input likely has enough points; still we should replicate.\n\nWe'll initialize gdist and glabel arrays to FLT_MAX/-1 before merge, and only fill first min(k,total) entries. Then copy labels into a new array `labels` of size k with -1 for unfilled slots. Then qsort and count as original.\n\nBut note: In original code, after insertion loop, distances array may contain some -1 if fewer than k points; then qsort(labels,k,sizeof(char),compare_for_sort) sorts including -1 values (which are negative). The frequency counting starts with most_frequent=labels[0] which could be -1. That's fine.\n\nWe'll replicate exactly: after merge, we have glabel array of size k but only first m entries valid where m = min(k,total). We need to set remaining to -1. Then copy to labels and qsort.\n\nBut careful: The original insertion algorithm uses sentinel -1 for distances; when a point with distance d arrives, it inserts at first position where d < distances[x] OR distances[x]==-1. Since all real distances are >=0, any -1 slot is considered \"empty\" and will be filled before later slots? Actually if there are multiple -1 slots, the first one gets filled because condition true at x=0 if distances[0]==-1; after filling, next point may fill next empty etc. So final list has real entries in first m positions (m = number of points processed up to k), and remaining positions still -1. Our merge should produce same: we select top-k by distance/idx; but if total < k, there are fewer than k real distances, so after selecting all real ones, the rest remain sentinel. In our merge loop, when no more real entries (best_t==-1), we break and leave remaining glabel as -1 (we initialized to -1). Good.\n\nNow frequency counting: We'll copy labels array from glabel (which has -1 for empty slots) then qsort and count exactly like original. But note: Original uses `char *labels = malloc(sizeof(char)*k);` and fills with -1 initially, then during insertion sets some to group label. After loop, it calls qsort(labels,k,sizeof(char),compare_for_sort). Our labels array after merge should be identical.\n\nLet's implement carefully.\n\nPotential performance issues: Allocating per-thread arrays inside knn each call; but main only calls once. Fine.\n\nWe also need to free memory? Not necessary for contest but good practice.\n\nNow we must ensure OpenMP is enabled in compilation. The Makefile likely uses -fopenmp? We don't control Makefile, but the environment probably compiles with appropriate flags based on source? Actually the controller builds using provided Makefile; we cannot change it. But if our code uses omp.h and pragmas, the build must include -fopenmp. The baseline may already have OpenMP enabled? Not sure. We can check previous experiments: they used OpenMP likely. Since role C is about OpenMP, the harness probably compiles with -fopenmp by default or we need to ensure it's in Makefile but we cannot edit Makefile (only src files). However instructions say \"Edit only source files under src/.\" So we can't change Makefile. But maybe the build system automatically adds -fopenmp when omp.h is included? Unlikely. We should check if previous successful experiments used OpenMP; they did, so likely the Makefile already has -fopenmp or compiler defaults to it. The baseline source didn't use OpenMP but still compiled. If we add #pragma omp, without -fopenmp it will be ignored (treated as comment) and code would compile sequentially with our parallel logic? Actually if not compiled with -fopenmp, `#pragma omp` is ignored, so the loop runs on one thread; then nthreads = omp_get_max_threads() returns 1 (if OpenMP disabled, it might return 1 or cause link error because omp functions are not available). If libgomp not linked, linking fails. So we need to ensure -fopenmp is used. Since previous experiments with role C succeeded and likely used OpenMP, the build environment must support it. We'll assume.\n\nAlternatively, we could use `_OPENMP` macro guard? But if not defined, omp_get_max_threads won't compile. Better to include <omp.h>; if compiler doesn't have -fopenmp, compilation fails because omp.h may still exist but functions unresolved at link. So likely the Makefile already has -fopenmp.\n\nWe'll proceed.\n\nNow we need to write complete files knn.c and knn.h with our changes.\n\nLet's craft final code.\n\nFirst knn.h: We'll replace parsing functions with fast versions. Keep euclidean_distance_no_sqrt and compare_for_sort unchanged.\n\nNeed to include <stdbool.h>? We can use int for seen flag.\n\nAlso note: The original parse_point uses scanf(\" (%f ,%f) \") which allows spaces around comma and parentheses. Our manual parser should handle similar whitespace flexibility. We'll skip_ws before each token.\n\nLet's write knn.h:\n\n```\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\ntypedef struct {\n    float x;\n    float y;\n} Point;\n\ntypedef struct {\n    char label;\n    int length;\n    Point * points;\n} Group;\n\nstatic char *input_data = NULL;\nstatic size_t input_pos = 0;\nstatic size_t input_len = 0;\n\nvoid on_error() {\n    printf(\"Invalid input file.\\n\");\n    exit(1);\n}\n\nstatic void read_all_input() {\n    if (input_data) return;\n    // Try fseek to get size\n    long sz = -1;\n    if (!fseek(stdin, 0, SEEK_END)) {\n        sz = ftell(stdin);\n        rewind(stdin);\n    }\n    if (sz > 0) {\n        input_data = (char*)malloc(sz ? sz : 1);\n        size_t r = fread(input_data, 1, sz, stdin);\n        if (r != (size_t)sz && !feof(stdin)) on_error();\n        input_len = r;\n    } else {\n        // fallback: read char by char\n        size_t cap = 1<<20;\n        input_data = (char*)malloc(cap);\n        int c; size_t n=0;\n        while ((c=getchar())!=EOF) {\n            if(n+1>=cap){cap*=2; input_data=(char*)realloc(input_data,cap);}\n            input_data[n++]=(char)c;\n        }\n        input_len=n;\n    }\n    input_pos=0;\n}\n\nstatic void skip_ws() {\n    while (input_pos < input_len) {\n        char c = input_data[input_pos];\n        if(c==' '||c=='\\n'||c=='\\t'||c=='\\r') input_pos++;\n        else break;\n    }\n}\n\nstatic int parse_int() {\n    skip_ws();\n    int sign=1, val=0; bool seen=false;\n    if(input_pos<input_len && (input_data[input_pos]=='-'||input_data[input_pos]=='+')){if(input_data[input_pos]=='-')sign=-1; input_pos++;}\n    while(input_pos<input_len && input_data[input_pos]>='0'&&input_data[input_pos]<='9'){\n        val=val*10+(input_data[input_pos]-'0'); input_pos++; seen=true;\n    }\n    if(!seen) on_error();\n    return sign*val;\n}\n\nstatic float parse_float() {\n    skip_ws();\n    int sign=1; double val=0.0; bool seen=false;\n    if(input_pos<input_len && (input_data[input_pos]=='-'||input_data[input_pos]=='+')){if(input_data[input_pos]=='-')sign=-1; input_pos++;}\n    while(input_pos<input_len && input_data[input_pos]>='0'&&input_data[input_pos]<='9'){\n        val=val*10+(input_data[input_pos]-'0'); input_pos++; seen=true;\n    }\n    if(input_pos<input_len && input_data[input_pos]=='.'){\n        input_pos++;\n        double frac=1.0;\n        while(input_pos<input_len && input_data[input_pos]>='0'&&input_data[input_pos]<='9'){\n            frac/=10; val += (input_data[input_pos]-'0')*frac; input_pos++; seen=true;\n        }\n    }\n    if(!seen) on_error();\n    return sign*(float)val;\n}\n\nint parse_number_of_groups() {\n    read_all_input();\n    skip_ws();\n    const char *needle = \"n_groups=\";\n    size_t nl = strlen(needle);\n    if (input_pos + nl > input_len || strncmp(input_data+input_pos, needle, nl)!=0) on_error();\n    input_pos += nl;\n    return parse_int();\n}\n\nPoint parse_point() {\n    read_all_input(); // ensure\n    skip_ws();\n    if(input_pos>=input_len || input_data[input_pos]!='(') on_error();\n    input_pos++;\n    float x = parse_float();\n    skip_ws();\n    if(input_pos>=input_len || input_data[input_pos]!=',') on_error();\n    input_pos++;\n    float y = parse_float();\n    skip_ws();\n    if(input_pos>=input_len || input_data[input_pos]!=')') on_error();\n    input_pos++;\n    Point p; p.x=x; p.y=y; return p;\n}\n\nGroup parse_next_group() {\n    read_all_input(); // ensure\n    char label;\n    skip_ws();\n    const char *lbl = \"label=\";\n    size_t ll = strlen(lbl);\n    if (input_pos + ll > input_len || strncmp(input_data+input_pos, lbl, ll)!=0) on_error();\n    input_pos += ll;\n    skip_ws();\n    if(input_pos>=input_len) on_error();\n    label = input_data[input_pos++];\n    \n    skip_ws();\n    const char *len_str = \"length=\";\n    size_t lls = strlen(len_str);\n    if (input_pos + lls > input_len || strncmp(input_data+input_pos, len_str, lls)!=0) on_error();\n    input_pos += lls;\n    int length = parse_int();\n\n    Group g; g.label=label; g.length=length;\n    g.points = (Point*)malloc(sizeof(Point)*length);\n    for(int i=0;i<length;i++) g.points[i]=parse_point();\n    return g;\n}\n\nint parse_k() {\n    read_all_input(); // ensure\n    skip_ws();\n    const char *needle = \"k=\";\n    size_t nl = strlen(needle);\n    if (input_pos + nl > input_len || strncmp(input_data+input_pos, needle, nl)!=0) on_error();\n    input_pos += nl;\n    return parse_int();\n}\n\nfloat euclidean_distance_no_sqrt (Point a, Point b) {\n    float dx = b.x - a.x;\n    float dy = b.y - a.y;\n    return dx*dx + dy*dy;\n}\n\nint compare_for_sort(const void *a, const void *b) {\n  return *(char*)a - *(char*)b;\n}\n```\n\nNeed to include <stdbool.h> for bool. Add `#include <stdbool.h>`.\n\nNow knn.c:\n\n```\n#include \"knn.h\"\n#include <omp.h>\n#include <float.h>\n\nchar knn(int n_groups, Group * groups, int k, Point to_evaluate) {\n    long total = 0;\n    for (int i=0;i<n_groups;i++) total += groups[i].length;\n\n    // Flatten points and labels\n    char *flat_labels = (char*)malloc(total ? total : 1);\n    Point *flat_points = (Point*)malloc(sizeof(Point)*(total?total:1));\n    long pos=0;\n    for(int gi=0;gi<n_groups;gi++){\n        Group g = groups[gi];\n        for(int pi=0;pi<g.length;pi++){\n            flat_labels[pos]=g.label;\n            flat_points[pos]=g.points[pi];\n            pos++;\n        }\n    }\n\n    int nthreads = omp_get_max_threads();\n    float **ldists = (float**)malloc(sizeof(float*)*nthreads);\n    char **llabels = (char**)malloc(sizeof(char*)*nthreads);\n    long **lidx = (long**)malloc(sizeof(long*)*nthreads);\n    for(int t=0;t<nthreads;t++){\n        ldists[t]=(float*)malloc(sizeof(float)*k);\n        llabels[t]=(char*)malloc(sizeof(char)*k);\n        lidx[t]=(long*)malloc(sizeof(long)*k);\n        for(int j=0;j<k;j++){ldists[t][j]=FLT_MAX; llabels[t][j]=-1; lidx[t][j]=-1;}\n    }\n\n    #pragma omp parallel\n    {\n        int tid = omp_get_thread_num();\n        float *ld = ldists[tid];\n        char *ll = llabels[tid];\n        long *li = lidx[tid];\n\n        #pragma omp for schedule(static)\n        for(long idx=0; idx<total; idx++){\n            float d = euclidean_distance_no_sqrt(to_evaluate, flat_points[idx]);\n            int x;\n            for(x=0;x<k;x++){\n                if(d < ld[x] || ld[x]==FLT_MAX){\n                    for(int y=k-1;y>x;y--){\n                        ld[y]=ld[y-1];\n                        ll[y]=ll[y-1];\n                        li[y]=li[y-1];\n                    }\n                    ld[x]=d;\n                    ll[x]=flat_labels[idx];\n                    li[x]=idx;\n                    break;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Merge per-thread lists\n    float *gdist = (float*)malloc(sizeof(float)*k);\n    char *glabel = (char*)malloc(sizeof(char)*k);\n    for(int i=0;i<k;i++){ gdist[i]=FLT_MAX; glabel[i]=-1; }\n    int *ptr = (int*)calloc(nthreads, sizeof(int));\n    int filled=0;\n    while(filled < k){\n        int best_t=-1;\n        float best_d=FLT_MAX;\n        long best_idx=-1;\n        for(int t=0;t<nthreads;t++){\n            if(ptr[t] < k && ldists[t][ptr[t]] != FLT_MAX){\n                float d = ldists[t][ptr[t]];\n                long idx = lidx[t][ptr[t]];\n                if(d < best_d || (d==best_d && idx < best_idx)){\n                    best_d=d; best_idx=idx; best_t=t;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        if(best_t==-1) break;\n        gdist[filled]=best_d;\n        glabel[filled]=llabels[best_t][ptr[best_t]];\n        ptr[best_t]++;\n        filled++;\n    }\n\n    // Build labels array exactly like original (with -1 for empty slots)\n    char *labels = (char*)malloc(sizeof(char)*k);\n    for(int i=0;i<k;i++) labels[i] = glabel[i]; // already initialized to -1\n\n    qsort(labels, k, sizeof(char), compare_for_sort);\n\n    char most_frequent = labels[0];\n    int most_frequent_count = 1;\n    int current_frequency = 1;\n\n    for (int i = 1; i < k; i++) {\n        if (labels[i] != labels[i - 1]) {\n            if (current_frequency > most_frequent_count) {\n                most_frequent = labels[i - 1];\n                most_frequent_count = current_frequency;\n            }\n            current_frequency = 1;\n        } else {\n            current_frequency++;\n        }\n\n        if (i == k - 1 && current_frequency > most_frequent_count) {\n            most_frequent = labels[i - 1];\n            most_frequent_count = current_frequency;\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Free memory? Not necessary but good\n    free(flat_labels); free(flat_points);\n    for(int t=0;t<nthreads;t++){free(ldists[t]); free(llabels[t]); free(lidx[t]);}\n    free(ldists); free(llabels); free(lidx);\n    free(gdist); free(glabel); free(ptr); free(labels);\n\n    return most_frequent;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    int n_groups = parse_number_of_groups();\n    \n    Group * groups = (Group *) malloc(sizeof(Group) * n_groups);\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < n_groups; i++) {\n        groups[i] = parse_next_group();\n    }\n\n    int k = parse_k();\n\n    Point to_evaluate = parse_point();\n\n    printf(\"%c\", knn(n_groups, groups, k, to_evaluate));\n}\n```\n\nPotential issues:\n\n- `omp_get_max_threads()` returns number of threads available; if OpenMP disabled it might return 1 but linking fails. We'll assume enabled.\n- If total is large and k small, per-thread insertion O(k) each point; fine.\n- The merge loop uses while(filled<k) with linear scan over nthreads (<=4), so O(nthreads*k).\n- Need to include <stdlib.h> already in knn.h.\n\nOne subtlety: In the parallel for, we use `schedule(static)` which divides iterations contiguously among threads. This ensures that each thread's subset is a contiguous range of indices, preserving original order within thread and across threads (thread0 gets first chunk). Our merge tie-breaking by idx works because global index order corresponds to processing order.\n\nBut what if OpenMP static schedule with default chunk size may not be perfectly balanced? It still assigns contiguous blocks; the exact partition doesn't matter as long as each thread's indices are a subset and within each thread insertion is stable. The merge compares idx globally, so it will pick correct global top-k regardless of how chunks split, because we consider all candidates from all threads and choose smallest (d,idx). So even if schedule(static) with different chunking, correctness holds.\n\nHowever, there is a subtle issue: Within each thread's local list, for equal distances, insertion order corresponds to increasing idx within that thread's subset. But when merging across threads, we compare idx directly; since all indices are unique and global order is total, the merge will correctly select smallest idx among ties. So fine.\n\nAnother potential issue: The original sequential algorithm uses `distances[x] == -1` as sentinel. Our local lists use FLT_MAX. When a point has distance exactly equal to some existing real distance, insertion condition d < ld[x] is false; if there's an empty slot later (FLT_MAX), it will insert there. This matches sequential because sequential also uses `distances[x]==-1` as sentinel and would insert at first -1 after all smaller/equal distances? Actually in sequential, if d equals existing distance at x=0, condition false; then checks x=1: if distances[1] is -1 (empty), it inserts there. So yes.\n\nNow we need to ensure that the merge produces exactly same labels as sequential even when k > total points. In that case, after processing all points, local lists may have fewer than k real entries; merge fills filled = min(k,total) and leaves rest glabel=-1. Then qsort sorts including -1s. Original also has -1 in remaining slots. Good.\n\nBut note: In original code, distances array is initialized to -1 for all k before processing points. If total < k, after loop some entries remain -1. The labels array also remains -1 for those positions. Then qsort sorts them; the frequency counting may produce most_frequent = -1 if no label appears more than once? But input likely has enough points.\n\nNow we should test mentally with example from statement: n_groups=4, k=5, total points 3+5+4+3=15 >5. Should work.\n\nPotential performance improvements: We could avoid flattening by using group offsets and parallelizing over groups or points directly without copying. But flattening is O(N) memory and time; acceptable.\n\nWe also need to ensure that `euclidean_distance_no_sqrt` uses float arithmetic exactly as original (it does). The compiler may use FMA etc but same expression.\n\nNow we must output JSON with files contents.\n\nLet's write final code carefully, ensuring no syntax errors.\n\nCheck knn.h: We used bool without including stdbool. Add `#include <stdbool.h>` at top.\n\nAlso note: In parse_number_of_groups, after reading \"n_groups=\", there may be spaces before number; parse_int handles skip_ws. Good.\n\nIn parse_next_group, after label char, we call skip_ws then expect \"length=\". But the input format has newline between label and length? Example:\n```\nlabel=A\nlength=3\n```\nSo after reading 'A', next char is '\\n'; skip_ws will consume it, then match \"length=\". Good.\n\nIn parse_point, after '(' we call parse_float which skips ws; but there might be no space between '(' and number? Example \"(-3.55,-1.28)\" so fine. After x, skip_ws before comma; if comma immediately follows, skip_ws does nothing. Then read y.\n\nOne issue: The original scanf format `\" (%f ,%f) \"` allows spaces around comma and parentheses. Our parser handles that by skipping whitespace at each step.\n\nNow knn.c includes <omp.h> and <float.h>. We also need to include <stdlib.h> already via knn.h.\n\nPotential issue: If total is 0, flat_points malloc(1) etc; loop won't execute; merge will fill none; labels all -1; qsort of k elements with -1; most_frequent = -1. Original would do same? If no points, distances remain -1 and labels -1; after qsort, most_frequent=-1. So fine.\n\nNow we need to ensure that the parallel region doesn't have race conditions: each thread writes only its own arrays. Good.\n\nOne more thing: The `#pragma omp for schedule(static)` inside a parallel region will distribute iterations among threads. By default there is an implicit barrier at end of loop, so all threads finish before merge (which is outside parallel region). Good.\n\nNow we should consider if using OpenMP with static scheduling and per-thread arrays might cause false sharing? Not significant.\n\nWe'll output the two files.\n\nLet's craft final JSON.\n\nHypothesis: \"Parallelize KNN top-k selection across points using OpenMP static work sharing, each thread maintaining a private sorted list; merge deterministically by (distance, original index) to preserve exact sequential tie-breaking. Replace scanf-based parsing with a custom buffered parser to reduce I/O overhead.\"\n\nTransformations:\n- Replaced scanf parsing with manual buffered parser.\n- Added OpenMP parallel for over flattened points with per-thread top-k lists.\n- Deterministic merge of per-thread lists preserving original order semantics.\n\nSources_kept: [] because integration_source_ids empty.\n\nNotes: mention that correctness relies on lexicographic ordering equivalence.\n\nNow produce JSON."
